WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.901
Hello Ornella.
Thank you very much

00:00:18.901 --> 00:00:24.201
for being part
of this workshop

00:00:24.201 --> 00:00:27.001
organized by
African Futures Action Lab

00:00:28.901 --> 00:00:31.901
in collaboration with
the members of CaCoBuRwa,

00:00:31.901 --> 00:00:36.601
the collective of Congolese, Burundian
and Rwandan associations.

00:00:36.701 --> 00:00:43.601
We asked you because we would like to...
we saw in the whole debate

00:00:44.801 --> 00:00:50.901
on the question of colonial memories,
of the way in which we can tackle the injustices

00:00:50.901 --> 00:00:53.001
of colonization,
the colonial crimes,

00:00:54.201 --> 00:00:59.901
we constantly need to
refer to the archives,

00:00:59.901 --> 00:01:07.301
we need to... it is an element...
The archival component is a central element.

00:01:07.301 --> 00:01:13.101
Hence, when you are not a historian,
you are not familiar with the archives.

00:01:14.201 --> 00:01:19.101
There are a lot of questions
that we ask ourselves

00:01:19.101 --> 00:01:24.001
and therefore we asked you,
as a historian who has worked

00:01:24.001 --> 00:01:31.901
and still works
on the colonial archives of Belgium,

00:01:31.901 --> 00:01:33.001
to enlighten us regarding
the complexity of the archives.

00:01:33.501 --> 00:01:37.401
Perhaps before we start
with a series of questions,

00:01:37.401 --> 00:01:41.401
can you tell us
a little bit

00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:42.801
about your background?

00:01:43.801 --> 00:01:47.801
Hello and thank you
very much for this invitation.

00:01:47.801 --> 00:01:53.201
I am a historian,
trained in contemporary history.

00:01:53.201 --> 00:01:59.901
In fact, my research is focused on the history
of justice and on the history of Rwanda,

00:01:59.901 --> 00:02:04.001
but also, more recently,
of Burundi and of Congo.

00:02:04.501 --> 00:02:07.201
My specialty
is judicial archives,

00:02:07.201 --> 00:02:13.301
which are in fact documents that historians -
as we were just talking about - are extremely enthusiastic about

00:02:13.301 --> 00:02:17.501
because these archives
offer a whole series of perspectives.

00:02:17.501 --> 00:02:21.501
One of the questions
that motivated my research

00:02:21.501 --> 00:02:24.501
is also to try to see what we can do
with these archives.

00:02:24.501 --> 00:02:30.001
I hope that there are points of association
with the debates that concern

00:02:30.001 --> 00:02:32.801
your seminar and your projects.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.701
Yes, great.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:48.701
In any case, we are delighted
to have you here.

00:02:50.001 --> 00:02:55.001
One of the questions that is
currently preoccupying us is to know,

00:02:56.001 --> 00:03:03.501
if we want to understand the crimes
committed during colonization,

00:03:03.501 --> 00:03:10.801
to what extent the path of the archives
is an appropriate way?

00:03:10.801 --> 00:03:15.801
To what extent
is it possible to go that way?

00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:19.901
What do we...
So the measures are costly.

00:03:20.101 --> 00:03:25.001
What does that entail
in terms of the narratives

00:03:25.001 --> 00:03:29.201
that we have access to?
To what extent is it limited?

00:03:29.201 --> 00:03:34.801
Can you start
with this first big question?

00:03:36.201 --> 00:03:42.901
Is this a way to understand
the injustices of colonization?

00:03:44.701 --> 00:03:50.501
The first element is to recall that the archives,
which are documents that were produced

00:03:50.501 --> 00:03:55.301
by public or private institutions,
or even individuals,

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:58.601
companies,
religious congregations,

00:03:58.801 --> 00:04:02.801
these archives, which will be
found on several media,

00:04:02.801 --> 00:04:06.001
can be written archives,
or iconographic documents.

00:04:06.991 --> 00:04:10.801
A whole series
of documents, maps...

00:04:11.001 --> 00:04:14.201
These archives
are the raw material of historians.

00:04:15.601 --> 00:04:20.101
It can also be oral archives.
Thus, I do not exclude the oral history from that, of course.

00:04:20.101 --> 00:04:23.301
What does this raw material
allow us to do?

00:04:23.301 --> 00:04:28.101
Well, it allows us
to observe the past.

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:31.801
This observation of the past
involves a whole series of questions.

00:04:31.801 --> 00:04:36.192
When we are faced with a document
that has been produced and preserved,

00:04:36.192 --> 00:04:38.001
it is perhaps a question to which
we will be able to come back;

00:04:38.001 --> 00:04:45.001
obviously, it is which documents are finally reaching us
today from this period of colonization?

00:04:45.001 --> 00:04:50.001
Well, when we are faced with this archive, we ask
a series of questions, we come up with a number of questions.

00:04:51.001 --> 00:04:55.801
The objective of this archive and its use

00:04:55.801 --> 00:05:01.001
will be to contribute to answering them
from a certain point of view.

00:05:01.001 --> 00:05:05.001
There were several questions
within this first question.

00:05:05.001 --> 00:05:11.701
That is to say that the archive is produced
by an archive producer who describes in a certain way

00:05:11.701 --> 00:05:15.001
the reality of that time,
but of course, it is a point of view.

00:05:15.001 --> 00:05:20.001
So the whole point of using archives
is to be aware of this standpoint, to deconstruct it,

00:05:20.001 --> 00:05:23.301
to question it and to confront it
with other points of view.

00:05:23.301 --> 00:05:28.001
This is what we call the crossing of sources.
A single archive will not give us

00:05:28.001 --> 00:05:32.001
direct access to the past
as it was at that time.

00:05:33.001 --> 00:05:39.501
It will give us access to a vision of this past,
which was the actual present at the time.

00:05:39.901 --> 00:05:46.001
Thus, to approach these questions,
we must not exclude

00:05:46.001 --> 00:05:50.501
all the other social and human sciences,
political sciences, jurists,

00:05:50.501 --> 00:05:53.601
anthropologists
who also use archives.

00:05:53.601 --> 00:05:57.501
Historians do
what is called historical criticism

00:05:57.601 --> 00:06:03.101
and it is a whole series of methods, tools,
and questioning that allow us

00:06:03.101 --> 00:06:07.701
to put this archive in context and try
to question it to the best of our ability.

00:06:07.701 --> 00:06:13.201
To answer the question: do archives
allow you to document or understand,

00:06:13.201 --> 00:06:20.501
to question the injustices
of the colonial past?

00:06:20.501 --> 00:06:26.001
It is certainly an essential element
in which we can find a certain number of answers

00:06:26.001 --> 00:06:31.601
because it is this raw material
that gives us access to this past.

00:06:34.001 --> 00:06:37.001
Now, as I was saying, everything also
depends on what we obtain.

00:06:37.001 --> 00:06:42.701
The archive is produced at a certain moment
and then it will be preserved or not.

00:06:43.201 --> 00:06:48.501
This conservation will depend
on intellectual decisions.

00:06:48.501 --> 00:06:52.001
Does this archive have sufficient
historical value to allow us to keep it?

00:06:52.101 --> 00:06:56.901
It will also depend, of course,
on the preservation and transmission conditions

00:06:56.901 --> 00:07:02.901
with the possibility of archives
that can be degraded, lost or destroyed.

00:07:02.901 --> 00:07:09.001
We had floods recently, in Belgium,
which had an impact on a certain number of archives

00:07:09.001 --> 00:07:11.101
that suffered,
that could have been destroyed.

00:07:11.101 --> 00:07:18.001
So there are voluntary and involuntary causes,
of course, of the destruction of these archives.

00:07:20.201 --> 00:07:26.001
So, public archives and private archives
reach us in a way

00:07:27.001 --> 00:07:28.901
that I would say
is sometimes not complete.

00:07:28.901 --> 00:07:32.801
If we now return to the specific
question of colonial archives,

00:07:32.801 --> 00:07:35.601
we are faced with another question:
these are archives that were produced

00:07:35.601 --> 00:07:37.201
in multiple places.

00:07:37.501 --> 00:07:44.001
We were talking about Congo, Burundi and Rwanda,
three geographical areas that have their own history,

00:07:44.201 --> 00:07:48.501
which therefore produced their own archives;
these archives were produced

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:53.901
in Africa, but also in Belgium.
Thus, we have collections that are split between the two.

00:07:53.901 --> 00:08:01.901
At the end of the 1950s, in fact, in 1960,
these archives were partially repatriated

00:08:01.901 --> 00:08:06.751
to Belgium. This means that we have documents
that have remained on site

00:08:06.751 --> 00:08:12.101
and we have documents that have been repatriated.
I think that we estimate that today, more or less,

00:08:12.101 --> 00:08:18.101
there are ten kilometers of so-called African archives,
colonial archives, they go by several names,

00:08:18.101 --> 00:08:22.101
that are kept in Belgium.

00:08:22.101 --> 00:08:26.301
Some documents were left behind,
some are still preserved today.

00:08:26.301 --> 00:08:32.001
Others have unfortunately disappeared
or have been destroyed over time.

00:08:32.501 --> 00:08:39.201
These colonial or African archives,
well, we'll see what term we use here,

00:08:39.801 --> 00:08:44.101
have one particularity, which is
that they are part of a sort of triptych.

00:08:44.101 --> 00:08:48.101
Between memory, justice and history.

00:08:48.101 --> 00:08:53.701
Clearly, the question that you ask
is the question of the mobilization

00:08:53.701 --> 00:08:59.001
of these archives and through justice,
as we saw with the Mestizo trial in Belgium.

00:08:59.001 --> 00:09:04.201
Through memory, we see it through collectives,
through what we call civil society.

00:09:04.201 --> 00:09:09.001
As well as through history, because historians
have been working on these archives for years.

00:09:09.501 --> 00:09:12.501
It is that nowadays, we see that there is
an extremely strong demand for history

00:09:12.501 --> 00:09:15.801
and that the discourse on history is carried
by many players, including non-historians.

00:09:15.801 --> 00:09:18.701
It is very good that way.

00:09:18.701 --> 00:09:21.601
Just to say that yes,
archives are fundamental,

00:09:21.601 --> 00:09:26.201
but they are not the only source of information
that allows us to observe this past, to question it,

00:09:26.201 --> 00:09:27.501
to understand it.

00:09:30.901 --> 00:09:35.001
Great. Perhaps to go
a little further

00:09:36.001 --> 00:09:39.901
in the questions,
in the elements that you address.

00:09:39.901 --> 00:09:43.201
You're describing
a situation in which

00:09:44.301 --> 00:09:48.401
archives are located
in several geographic spaces.

00:09:50.301 --> 00:09:55.901
The archives are both...
There has been a whole process

00:09:55.901 --> 00:10:00.001
of so-called sorting, between...

00:10:00.001 --> 00:10:04.101
the moment when
the document was made

00:10:04.301 --> 00:10:08.701
and contains a certain point of view,
the document was archived,

00:10:08.701 --> 00:10:13.801
the document, we decide whether to keep it
and all the hazards that can...

00:10:16.901 --> 00:10:19.701
that can arise afterwards.

00:10:21.201 --> 00:10:24.701
As a result,
I say to myself,

00:10:25.001 --> 00:10:30.001
in this whole process
where we have partial

00:10:30.501 --> 00:10:32.001
and biased views,

00:10:33.801 --> 00:10:38.001
what do we...
what do we have...

00:10:38.001 --> 00:10:41.201
what do we learn,
amongst other things, about...

00:10:43.601 --> 00:10:49.301
I mean, is there a view that emerges
on how these archives were...

00:10:49.401 --> 00:10:53.701
well, on the narratives that emerge,
where it was treated or is it that...

00:10:54.001 --> 00:10:56.701
we are still examining them

00:10:58.001 --> 00:11:02.001
and therefore,
we can't say...

00:11:03.701 --> 00:11:09.501
it's impossible to make an inventory of the injustices
of colonization from what has been treated

00:11:09.501 --> 00:11:10.501
until now?

00:11:11.801 --> 00:11:15.001
There are several things, I would say.
The first point

00:11:15.501 --> 00:11:20.001
is that this question of points of view
and of what can be drawn from an archive

00:11:20.001 --> 00:11:24.301
is a fundamental question for historians.

00:11:24.301 --> 00:11:28.001
Now, I'm talking about my point of view.
It is the one from which I can speak.

00:11:29.201 --> 00:11:32.901
It's true that the first question we ask ourselves,
I'll take an example from the court records,

00:11:32.901 --> 00:11:36.401
because they are the ones I know best
and where I can give concrete examples.

00:11:36.401 --> 00:11:40.101
When we are faced with a court file,
we say to ourselves: OK,

00:11:41.001 --> 00:11:44.901
in what context was this file produced?
What are the elements I need

00:11:44.901 --> 00:11:46.801
to understand what is going on?

00:11:46.801 --> 00:11:51.501
It's not only the context,
it's also to understand the language

00:11:51.501 --> 00:11:54.001
of the person who produced
the file at the time?

00:11:54.001 --> 00:11:58.201
Who are the players?
What is the architecture, in this case,

00:11:58.201 --> 00:12:00.401
of justice such as it functions?

00:12:00.401 --> 00:12:04.201
Once I am in front of my file,
what do I find in it?

00:12:04.401 --> 00:12:09.301
The judicial archives are a good example
to ask ourselves this question of injustices

00:12:09.501 --> 00:12:15.501
and of the way in which,
in this case, the Congolese,

00:12:15.501 --> 00:12:19.881
this is the example I have in mind,
interact with the system of justice

00:12:19.881 --> 00:12:21.981
that is actually a colonial justice.

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:28.581
What we find in a judicial file
are typically testimonies,

00:12:28.581 --> 00:12:33.801
confrontations of points of view
and the determination of facts.

00:12:33.801 --> 00:12:39.001
Therefore, it is obviously a fascinating field
for observing the question of violence,

00:12:39.001 --> 00:12:42.001
for observing
the question of interactions

00:12:42.601 --> 00:12:46.201
between the colonized
and the colonial authorities,

00:12:46.801 --> 00:12:52.001
for understanding the way
in which the law is constructed and hence

00:12:52.501 --> 00:12:59.001
a form of violence in the sense that the law,
as we know, does not apply in the same way

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:04.001
to whites as to Congolese
if we take this example in a colonial context.

00:13:04.001 --> 00:13:08.101
We have specific offences that are defined
in a specific way with specific penalties.

00:13:08.101 --> 00:13:14.201
We really have an asymmetrical system
that is actually reflected in these judicial records.

00:13:15.501 --> 00:13:21.001
That's one thing,
this entire legal framework

00:13:21.001 --> 00:13:22.001
that allows us to question it.

00:13:22.001 --> 00:13:26.401
Then there is the other aspect,
which is transversal to all court records

00:13:26.401 --> 00:13:27.901
and that is the question of testimony.

00:13:27.901 --> 00:13:32.901
When it comes to the question of testimony,
the historian says to himself : “OK, here I will have access

00:13:32.901 --> 00:13:35.601
to the voice of the players.”

00:13:35.601 --> 00:13:40.601
The voice of the players is also the voice
of the victims, or the voice of the people

00:13:40.601 --> 00:13:44.701
who were convicted or prosecuted,
like the accused.

00:13:46.201 --> 00:13:52.001
It's extremely exhilarating because we say to ourselves
that we have this capacity for direct observation

00:13:52.001 --> 00:13:56.301
to be able to use the expression
of Mary Bloch, a historian, who speaks

00:13:56.301 --> 00:14:01.001
of this historical observation by saying :
“The historian will never be an anthropologist.”

00:14:01.201 --> 00:14:04.001
He will not be able to go and do fieldwork.
Obviously, we can temper this

00:14:04.001 --> 00:14:06.101
because today,
this is the case

00:14:06.801 --> 00:14:09.801
but all he can do is this historical
observation through documents.

00:14:09.801 --> 00:14:12.081
We're caught up in it.
We say to ourselves: “Yes, with the testimony,

00:14:12.081 --> 00:14:16.501
I have access to the voice of the person.”
Obviously, when we talk about judicial testimony,

00:14:16.501 --> 00:14:20.501
even archives produced by other types
of public or state institutions,

00:14:20.501 --> 00:14:22.001
this voice is filtered.

00:14:22.001 --> 00:14:25.901
It is filtered in different ways.
First, it is transcribed.

00:14:26.101 --> 00:14:28.001
In this case, we are talking
about written documents?

00:14:28.001 --> 00:14:31.801
Who transcribed?
In what language was this person speaking?

00:14:31.801 --> 00:14:34.501
It is unlikely that this person
was speaking in French at the time.

00:14:34.501 --> 00:14:38.001
However, today, what we read
is a transcription in French.

00:14:38.901 --> 00:14:42.001
How was his statement
summarized, interpreted?

00:14:42.001 --> 00:14:47.001
Is it consistent with what was said?
Some of these questions are impossible

00:14:47.001 --> 00:14:50.001
to resolve, obviously,
for the historian today,

00:14:50.001 --> 00:14:55.001
but taking them into account and trying
to find information from even the smallest details

00:14:55.001 --> 00:15:00.001
of these testimonies makes it possible
to overcome all the biases

00:15:00.001 --> 00:15:05.701
that any archive contains, and this is not
specific to colonial or African archives.

00:15:05.701 --> 00:15:11.701
To be able to try to find
this original voice and to put it in relation

00:15:11.701 --> 00:15:16.501
with other types of archives,
we could think of oral history campaigns

00:15:16.501 --> 00:15:19.501
for more recent periods
that can put this into perspective.

00:15:19.501 --> 00:15:23.801
These are things that are being done,
it's not... well it's to be done, of course,

00:15:23.801 --> 00:15:26.201
but it's already being done.

00:15:27.101 --> 00:15:32.001
So here we are, a judicial file
or an archive file in general

00:15:32.001 --> 00:15:38.001
is a document that was produced at a specific moment,
that has its own history and that, finally,

00:15:38.001 --> 00:15:41.901
lives again through our own questioning.
I always come back to this question

00:15:41.901 --> 00:15:46.201
because I think it is essential.
Of course, there are things that are given in an archive.

00:15:46.201 --> 00:15:48.101
The archive is dated.

00:15:48.701 --> 00:15:52.801
It contains names,
it contains a place.

00:15:53.501 --> 00:16:00.001
It contains, maybe, a description of the facts,
such and such event took place in the military camp of...

00:16:00.001 --> 00:16:04.601
the soldier is interrogated
because he is accused of... etc.

00:16:04.601 --> 00:16:07.901
In spite of everything, beyond that,
what the archive will be able to deliver

00:16:07.901 --> 00:16:11.401
is obviously the questions that we have.
There, it is not only the historians who come

00:16:11.401 --> 00:16:14.201
with questions towards the documents,
but they are also the citizens

00:16:14.201 --> 00:16:19.001
because the archive
is part of a democratic, state system.

00:16:19.001 --> 00:16:23.801
Therefore, everyone must be able
to have access to it for various reasons.

00:16:23.801 --> 00:16:30.201
The research is one of them.
Genealogical questions is another one.

00:16:30.201 --> 00:16:35.201
Problems of ownership...
There is a whole series of questions... and reasons,

00:16:35.201 --> 00:16:39.851
that can lead a person
to want to consult an archive.

00:16:44.701 --> 00:16:50.001
I have two other questions.
I don't know if I separate them or if I...

00:16:51.801 --> 00:16:54.501
Maybe I'll start with one question
and come back to the other afterwards.

00:16:54.501 --> 00:16:56.501
The first one is :

00:16:58.501 --> 00:17:07.001
as an ordinary citizen, if we want
to try to understand a little

00:17:07.001 --> 00:17:11.501
or at least to look into what has been done,
in the Belgian archives,

00:17:11.501 --> 00:17:16.800
what are we faced with?
To take up what you were saying earlier,

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:23.601
the example given of the trial
of the Mestizo during colonization,

00:17:23.601 --> 00:17:30.901
the issue in the trial was, well,
one of the issues at stake was access to the archives

00:17:30.901 --> 00:17:33.001
and access
to their administrative documents.

00:17:33.001 --> 00:17:40.801
So the difficulty was, at least
for all these children who are now adults,

00:17:40.801 --> 00:17:43.801
to have access
and to understand what is in it.

00:17:43.801 --> 00:17:46.001
Therefore...

00:17:46.401 --> 00:17:51.801
even with a set of,
in their case,

00:17:52.801 --> 00:17:58.001
legal experts who were supporting them,
it felt like it was a daunting task.

00:17:58.701 --> 00:18:02.501
Now what state are they in?

00:18:03.001 --> 00:18:10.001
If an average citizen wants to tackle it,
what are the obstacles?

00:18:11.301 --> 00:18:14.201
Well, the obstacles for accessing
the archives can be multiple indeed.

00:18:14.201 --> 00:18:19.201
Of course, we can talk about the official position
of the opening of archives, but then there is the...

00:18:19.201 --> 00:18:21.901
the reality and the difficulties
to access them.

00:18:21.901 --> 00:18:25.701
Just one more remark before answering
this question about access in general.

00:18:25.701 --> 00:18:31.701
All archives, whether they are produced
by the State or whether they are private

00:18:31.701 --> 00:18:36.401
or by individuals, they always have what is called
a legal time limit of opening.

00:18:36.401 --> 00:18:40.101
That is to say, that after a certain number of years,
an archive becomes public.

00:18:40.701 --> 00:18:43.601
Often, this period is 30 years
for everything that concerns, for example,

00:18:43.601 --> 00:18:48.301
what is produced by the State.
Of course, to this 30-year period apply

00:18:49.001 --> 00:18:54.701
as many exceptions related to the question of privacy,
related to the question of the classification of documents.

00:18:54.701 --> 00:18:58.201
All this is important
to answer the specific question.

00:18:58.201 --> 00:19:01.801
If we take court records,
it is 100 years.

00:19:01.801 --> 00:19:05.701
It is said that after 100 years,
a judicial archive is public.

00:19:05.701 --> 00:19:09.231
So, do we say to ourselves : “Oh, we don't have access to anything at all.”

00:19:09.231 --> 00:19:13.247
Of course we do. There are exceptions in the other direction
like exemptions from law to access.

00:19:13.247 --> 00:19:17.547
This is typically what researchers do
to have access to documents.

00:19:17.547 --> 00:19:23.547
They have to make a request to obtain authorization.
This request may be addressed

00:19:23.547 --> 00:19:29.301
to the Archivist General of the Kingdom,
or it may, in the case of certain court records,

00:19:29.301 --> 00:19:32.001
be addressed to the College
of Public Prosecutors.

00:19:32.001 --> 00:19:35.701
For example, on a previous project,
to be able to access documents

00:19:35.701 --> 00:19:40.901
that were less than 100 years old and that dealt
with war crimes trials in Belgium,

00:19:40.901 --> 00:19:45.101
I had to make a request to the College
of Public Prosecutors, saying : “ Here we are,

00:19:45.101 --> 00:19:50.001
for research reasons with those objectives,
I would like to access such and such documents. ”

00:19:50.001 --> 00:19:52.901
It's a process that takes a certain amount of time
and then they give you access.

00:19:52.901 --> 00:19:58.001
Now, the problem,
more specifically with African archives,

00:19:58.001 --> 00:20:00.701
is that, as we said, these are archives
that have already travelled,

00:20:01.001 --> 00:20:05.401
in some instances.
They were “repatriated” in 1960.

00:20:05.401 --> 00:20:10.901
They have had a rather eventful history of conservation,
which has gone hand in hand with a history of,

00:20:10.901 --> 00:20:17.001
I would say,
rather marked opacity.

00:20:17.001 --> 00:20:22.401
These archives, like the archives
of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs

00:20:22.401 --> 00:20:26.501
and the archives of the Ministry of Defense, were in fact
considered to be, so-called sensitive archives

00:20:26.501 --> 00:20:33.001
that could possibly present a certain danger
if they were accessed for the sake of order

00:20:33.001 --> 00:20:38.001
in society, etc. Well, these are
obviously statements...

00:20:38.001 --> 00:20:41.301
Well, I put everything in quotes,
but there it is.

00:20:41.301 --> 00:20:46.501
This means that for a very long time,
these archives were quite closed,

00:20:46.501 --> 00:20:54.001
including to research, but certainly
to ordinary citizens, as you just said.

00:20:56.901 --> 00:21:00.901
One of the obstacles
to the opening of these archives,

00:21:00.901 --> 00:21:05.901
even from the researcher's point of view,
without going into the question of mobilization

00:21:05.901 --> 00:21:10.401
by civil society or by individuals,
was that we lacked the tools.

00:21:10.401 --> 00:21:16.601
We lacked the tools to know what existed,
where it was located, how extensive the documents were,

00:21:18.401 --> 00:21:21.901
what they contained.
In short, we lacked an inventory.

00:21:22.101 --> 00:21:28.201
An inventory or a database that would
make it possible to locate, target and request access

00:21:28.201 --> 00:21:30.501
to such and such documents
to answer certain questions.

00:21:30.501 --> 00:21:33.333
Obviously, the problem
is exactly the same

00:21:33.333 --> 00:21:35.901
for individuals who would like
to access these documents.

00:21:35.901 --> 00:21:40.501
So today, we are in a rather different phase,
and here I refer

00:21:40.501 --> 00:21:46.301
to the work of my colleagues on the Mestizo project
and to the general archives of the kingdom,

00:21:46.301 --> 00:21:52.001
which have published a guide
on the so-called African and colonial archives

00:21:53.001 --> 00:21:55.801
in Belgium, which is a guide that is,
I think, 2,000 pages long

00:21:55.801 --> 00:22:00.801
and which is accessible
to the public

00:22:00.801 --> 00:22:04.901
on the Internet,
and I can provide you with the link.

00:22:04.901 --> 00:22:07.901
It explains all of these steps
and says that now what is happening is that

00:22:07.901 --> 00:22:14.001
all of these archives that were kept
at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, which filtered,

00:22:14.501 --> 00:22:18.901
and it is they who use the word, my archival colleagues:
“a little in an arbitrary form”,

00:22:18.901 --> 00:22:22.701
the documents to which access
was given, are now being repatriated

00:22:22.701 --> 00:22:24.001
to the State archives.

00:22:24.001 --> 00:22:28.901
This repatriation to the State archives
clearly marks a break with the past

00:22:28.901 --> 00:22:32.801
in the sense that the State,
their mission, their first mission,

00:22:32.801 --> 00:22:35.101
is to make documents accessible.

00:22:35.101 --> 00:22:39.501
Making documents accessible means putting
archivists and researchers on these collections

00:22:39.501 --> 00:22:44.301
to study them, to chart them,
to compile inventories,

00:22:44.301 --> 00:22:50.001
and to give the public and researchers access
to these documents transparently, because that is

00:22:50.001 --> 00:22:54.001
obviously one of the challenges.
It is, finally, equal access to the archives

00:22:54.001 --> 00:22:56.501
in a transparent way.

00:22:56.681 --> 00:23:02.701
This does not mean that
when an individual, a citizen, has a question

00:23:02.701 --> 00:23:06.001
about his family, for example,
he can go to the archives

00:23:06.001 --> 00:23:10.501
and they will pull a file from the shelf
that will give all the answers.

00:23:10.501 --> 00:23:14.801
Obviously, when you come
with these questions

00:23:14.801 --> 00:23:21.101
about your past, you will be confronted
with a whole series of potential sources

00:23:21.101 --> 00:23:26.101
that may provide elements
or even just clues.

00:23:26.101 --> 00:23:32.001
That is one of the difficulties, obviously,
and archivists are there to accompany

00:23:33.801 --> 00:23:37.401
researchers and citizens
in this quest.

00:23:37.401 --> 00:23:42.501
However, it's true that this Mestizo trial,
which stems from the Mestizo Resolution was adopted

00:23:42.501 --> 00:23:45.851
by the House of Representatives.
Therefore, it was a political decision

00:23:46.001 --> 00:23:51.101
at one point to say: “There is a problem with the opening
of the archives, and it must be resolved.”

00:23:51.101 --> 00:23:55.501
These archives
must be made accessible.

00:23:55.601 --> 00:23:58.501
This debate on the accessibility
of the archives can be found elsewhere.

00:23:58.501 --> 00:24:01.201
I mean, we can look at what is happening
in France in relation to the archives

00:24:01.201 --> 00:24:06.501
on the relationship between France and Rwanda.
It's exactly the same issues of transparency

00:24:07.501 --> 00:24:12.501
and equal access to these archives,
whereas for the moment, we are still

00:24:12.501 --> 00:24:15.981
in a rather obscure situation
on the French side.

00:24:21.181 --> 00:24:25.001
Yes, I find it interesting,
what you are saying because in fact, it is...

00:24:25.501 --> 00:24:30.001
well, this question of access...

00:24:30.001 --> 00:24:35.501
is not only a technical issue,
it is above all a political question.

00:24:37.301 --> 00:24:41.501
So to speak of equal access,
I find that this term is quite significant

00:24:41.501 --> 00:24:45.201
in the way that it reflects

00:24:45.201 --> 00:24:48.401
these obstacles

00:24:48.401 --> 00:24:51.401
and the...

00:24:51.401 --> 00:24:58.101
and what limitation it can have
to be able to understand what there is in the archives.

00:25:00.800 --> 00:25:02.801
I had a little question
actually in relation to that.

00:25:02.801 --> 00:25:05.801
I was wondering if the...

00:25:07.001 --> 00:25:11.001
Does the fact that these archives
have been kept for a long time...

00:25:14.001 --> 00:25:18.501
I don't know if we can use the term “hidden”
but in any case inaccessible,

00:25:20.001 --> 00:25:25.001
can explain... In fact, does it have
anything to do with the...

00:25:25.001 --> 00:25:30.001
well, it helps to explain part of the way
Belgium has dealt with its colonial past?

00:25:31.701 --> 00:25:35.901
By that I mean the fact that there's...
I don't think there's much...

00:25:37.201 --> 00:25:38.401
There are not many...

00:25:39.801 --> 00:25:44.051
history of colonization
that is taught in schools.

00:25:44.551 --> 00:25:47.801
It's really very recent.
So, is there a link

00:25:47.801 --> 00:25:54.501
between this inaccessibility and the fact
that it has remained a hidden history for a long time?

00:25:54.501 --> 00:25:57.501
Well, at least not...

00:25:59.501 --> 00:26:02.001
not really present in society.

00:26:03.501 --> 00:26:08.501
Yes, there certainly is. I come back
to this triptych of justice, history and memory,

00:26:08.901 --> 00:26:13.401
which in the end reflects the different aspects
that come into play in this question.

00:26:13.401 --> 00:26:19.501
That is to say, politics,
society in general, justice in the sense that,

00:26:19.501 --> 00:26:25.101
in certain cases, reparations can be demanded,
as is the case in the Mestizo trial.

00:26:25.101 --> 00:26:28.101
However, we know
of other debates...

00:26:29.201 --> 00:26:33.901
in other formerly colonized spaces
where this question may arise.

00:26:33.901 --> 00:26:36.201
We have thought of slavery, etc.
Well, this is it.

00:26:37.801 --> 00:26:41.201
So, to speak of absen... sorry.

00:26:41.201 --> 00:26:43.201
I'm reiterating my idea,
it's not very clear.

00:26:43.901 --> 00:26:48.001
So we have this triptych and in this triptych,
I think that there is also...

00:26:48.701 --> 00:26:52.701
not only memory, but there is also
research that already exists.

00:26:52.701 --> 00:26:57.001
To say that, since 1960, there has been
no research, obviously, would be...

00:26:57.201 --> 00:27:02.801
it would be unfair to all the historians,
anthropologists and political scientists,

00:27:02.801 --> 00:27:05.501
who have worked on these questions.
Now the question is, how does

00:27:05.501 --> 00:27:08.501
this research find
its way into society?

00:27:08.501 --> 00:27:14.501
How are they received, financed, supported?
This is where politics comes in, obviously.

00:27:14.501 --> 00:27:17.001
Now, what we often see,
well, what I observe

00:27:17.001 --> 00:27:21.901
because it's a question that I find interesting
in all the commission studies on the past...

00:27:22.201 --> 00:27:27.501
once again in very diverse contexts, is that
sometimes the politicians come and place

00:27:27.501 --> 00:27:34.001
some sort of order to look into questions
that have already been dealt with by the researchers.

00:27:35.001 --> 00:27:41.301
However, it is true that these commissions
are important, because they allow to put the spotlight

00:27:41.301 --> 00:27:46.301
on these questions, and to actually
open the debate in society.

00:27:46.301 --> 00:27:51.101
Education is typically one
of the most important channels

00:27:51.101 --> 00:27:54.101
for dealing with these issues,
from school onwards.

00:27:54.101 --> 00:27:58.101
I think that afterwards, the situations
are very different from one school to another,

00:27:58.101 --> 00:28:01.101
that now, there are things
that are changing.

00:28:02.701 --> 00:28:08.401
However, it is certainly a combination, that the archives...
well, they weren't hidden, it was possible to access them,

00:28:08.401 --> 00:28:12.001
but it's true, for the reasons
we've already described,

00:28:12.001 --> 00:28:13.501
that it was very complicated.

00:28:14.501 --> 00:28:19.001
It wasn't exactly a message of openness
to say: “let's talk about this past,

00:28:19.001 --> 00:28:23.101
we are really
interested, politically,

00:28:23.101 --> 00:28:25.901
in coming to terms
with the past”, etc.

00:28:26.001 --> 00:28:29.601
So yes, clearly,
it's part of the process,

00:28:29.701 --> 00:28:33.201
not of silence,
but I would say

00:28:34.201 --> 00:28:39.501
of silence that is a bit variable,
or more or less hidden,

00:28:39.501 --> 00:28:43.101
but again, that has been dealt
with by historians for decades already.

00:28:47.801 --> 00:28:52.901
Another small question.
You mentioned the archives

00:28:52.901 --> 00:28:55.801
in Rwanda, Burundi and the DRC,

00:28:56.101 --> 00:29:00.001
archives from the colonial period.

00:29:00.001 --> 00:29:03.501
What is there in these archives?
Is there... What are we...

00:29:03.501 --> 00:29:08.501
To what extent
can they be used?

00:29:09.401 --> 00:29:12.001
So, yes...

00:29:12.001 --> 00:29:16.701
I think they are mobilizable...
There are big differences, I think,

00:29:16.701 --> 00:29:20.951
between the different collections.
So, personally, I haven't worked

00:29:20.951 --> 00:29:25.451
on these archives, from that time,
in these three countries, in the field,

00:29:25.451 --> 00:29:29.251
so I can't say exactly
what their contents are either,

00:29:29.251 --> 00:29:32.551
because I don't have
personal experience of that.

00:29:32.551 --> 00:29:37.051
There is a series of preserved archives
that were produced during the colonial period.

00:29:38.001 --> 00:29:41.501
Local archives
produced by local authorities.

00:29:42.001 --> 00:29:45.901
I know that there are still archives,
particularly on the question of the police force

00:29:45.901 --> 00:29:48.001
if we take the Congo,
well, the DRC.

00:29:48.001 --> 00:29:53.001
So that was the colonial army,
so a series of files that are still kept on site.

00:29:53.001 --> 00:29:57.701
My two colleagues on the current project on which I am working
have just returned from a mission there,

00:29:57.701 --> 00:30:01.001
and were able to see
these collections.

00:30:01.201 --> 00:30:05.901
In Burundi, we also have a series of documents
that are in the national archives.

00:30:05.901 --> 00:30:08.101
Obviously, these three countries
have national archives

00:30:08.101 --> 00:30:12.001
that are facing many material
and financial difficulties,

00:30:12.001 --> 00:30:18.501
and have also suffered
from crises, conflicts and wars

00:30:19.001 --> 00:30:21.201
in recent decades.

00:30:21.401 --> 00:30:25.901
So there are documents, which are not
extremely numerous, I think,

00:30:25.901 --> 00:30:30.501
but there are indeed documents
in these three centers,

00:30:30.801 --> 00:30:32.201
produced at different levels.

00:30:32.201 --> 00:30:36.201
Then there are all the archives
of the religious congregations.

00:30:36.901 --> 00:30:42.001
There too, for example for Rwanda,
there we can think of Kabgayi,

00:30:42.901 --> 00:30:47.001
the bishop's residence where
there are documents dating from that time.

00:30:47.001 --> 00:30:53.501
So we really have both national archives
and decentralized archives in these countries.

00:30:53.501 --> 00:30:57.001
This breaks the image of “there are no documents”,
because we often say that.

00:30:57.001 --> 00:31:01.401
Finally, to make the history,
to take in the facts, in a general way, of Africa.

00:31:01.401 --> 00:31:05.001
Yes, but “we have no documents”,
that is totally false, obviously.

00:31:05.001 --> 00:31:10.401
There are a lot of documents that exist, have existed
and that unfortunately, sometimes have disappeared.

00:31:10.401 --> 00:31:15.201
However, we can really
reject the idea that there are no documents.

00:31:15.201 --> 00:31:18.401
If we take the example
of Rwanda, obviously in '94,

00:31:18.401 --> 00:31:23.901
there was a great loss of a very large
number of documents that date

00:31:24.501 --> 00:31:30.001
perhaps more from the post-colonial period,
but, I think, also from the colonial period,

00:31:30.001 --> 00:31:36.001
which suffered from destruction,
displacement, theft, looting, etc.

00:31:42.801 --> 00:31:45.002
I'm going to ask you
a very last question

00:31:47.002 --> 00:31:48.802
that is a little...

00:31:50.002 --> 00:31:55.202
it's not the easiest
or most convenient.

00:31:58.002 --> 00:32:02.602
In fact, I have the impression
that we are on these...

00:32:02.702 --> 00:32:05.602
well, that we are
in these debates on...

00:32:06.002 --> 00:32:10.203
on questions of memory and that we realize
to what extent, this is a feeling I had,

00:32:10.203 --> 00:32:16.203
an impression I had when I attended
the trial of the Mestizo women.

00:32:16.203 --> 00:32:19.203
It is to see to what extent these...

00:32:20.803 --> 00:32:24.803
historians and archives
in this context,

00:32:26.003 --> 00:32:27.803
have a central role.

00:32:28.503 --> 00:32:33.403
It is extremely central, both to allow access
and to be able to say what's inside.

00:32:33.403 --> 00:32:37.003
Therefore, there is a kind
of role of guardian...

00:32:39.503 --> 00:32:42.803
yes, of guardian to a certain...

00:32:42.803 --> 00:32:48.003
well, to allow access to a certain memory,
to certain realities that many people ignore.

00:32:48.003 --> 00:32:51.003
There is an extremely...

00:32:51.703 --> 00:32:54.003
well, whether we like it or not,
very political role.

00:32:55.003 --> 00:32:59.503
That's the first element.
A second element that we talked about

00:32:59.503 --> 00:33:04.003
is to see to what extent,
in fact, all of this...

00:33:04.503 --> 00:33:06.503
as you were saying,

00:33:09.903 --> 00:33:15.003
the content of the archives is a given point
of view, at a given time, in a given context,

00:33:16.503 --> 00:33:22.103
and here, more in a colonial context with all
the symmetry of power that we are aware of.

00:33:22.403 --> 00:33:26.103
I have the impression that
from the point of view of the people who...

00:33:27.203 --> 00:33:29.303
who are, well who...

00:33:31.903 --> 00:33:34.003
who are mobilizing...

00:33:35.003 --> 00:33:40.003
on these... well, in any case
on these questions, we are...

00:33:40.903 --> 00:33:44.303
we have to rely on...

00:33:45.803 --> 00:33:51.003
well, it's a bit... I'm doing the same...
I'm thinking, but I'm making the same analogy

00:33:51.003 --> 00:33:56.003
with the law. I have the impression that we need
the law, but at the same time, it's not what...

00:33:56.003 --> 00:33:58.704
it's not the tool
that simplifies our lives.

00:33:59.904 --> 00:34:04.904
So, we need jurists, they have a political role,
but there is a whole legislative apparatus

00:34:04.904 --> 00:34:10.704
that in fact allows us to justify
and give a certain point of view

00:34:11.704 --> 00:34:16.714
of the violence of colonization.
So how do you, as a historian,

00:34:16.714 --> 00:34:22.014
place yourself in this?
Because there's something about... Okay...

00:34:22.014 --> 00:34:26.514
there is a political role,

00:34:26.514 --> 00:34:30.813
but at the same time, people are,
well many historians are saying:

00:34:30.813 --> 00:34:34.813
“Well, in fact, we have...”
Well, that's also true of...

00:34:35.813 --> 00:34:38.214
we're going to have a neutral position

00:34:40.014 --> 00:34:46.004
to work on documents on which
we try to bring out a neutral message.

00:34:46.004 --> 00:34:49.904
However, no, there is nothing
neutral in this story.

00:34:49.904 --> 00:34:55.304
So, my question is a bit scattered,
but I think you see what you...

00:34:55.304 --> 00:34:59.204
well, you see what I mean,
this sort of positioning

00:35:00.503 --> 00:35:06.004
in a debate that is
fortunately political,

00:35:06.004 --> 00:35:09.004
but where this...

00:35:10.804 --> 00:35:13.504
this presence of history is
at the same time a guarantee, well, a necessity,

00:35:13.504 --> 00:35:18.204
but at the same time a danger,
I think, both for you,

00:35:18.204 --> 00:35:19.504
but also for the people
who rely on to it.

00:35:20.404 --> 00:35:23.504
Yes, the question is, do they need me or not?
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:24.004 --> 00:35:26.604
Not so much...
No, not at all. I'm just kidding. No, no.

00:35:26.604 --> 00:35:30.204
From me, definitely not,
as an individual, that is for sure.

00:35:30.204 --> 00:35:33.204
But... I think
it's a very important question because...

00:35:33.204 --> 00:35:36.504
It's true that
I don't call it “political”...

00:35:38.304 --> 00:35:41.504
You can call it “engaged”.

00:35:41.504 --> 00:35:45.504
It's a fundamental question,
because it's been a while

00:35:45.504 --> 00:35:50.504
since historians are no longer, well,
most of them are no longer in the positivist idea

00:35:50.504 --> 00:35:54.504
of objectivity,
complete neutrality, etc.

00:35:55.004 --> 00:35:58.004
History, like all the other disciplines
in the humanities and social sciences,

00:35:59.004 --> 00:36:04.704
has integrated the fact that our point of view,
our analysis, is constructed in a given context.

00:36:05.004 --> 00:36:09.204
We live in our time,
so we are not... Well, I say “we”,

00:36:09.204 --> 00:36:15.004
I don't want to speak for everyone else.
I am not disconnected from realities.

00:36:15.004 --> 00:36:18.504
Besides, these realities, they interest me,
they fuel me on a daily basis.

00:36:18.504 --> 00:36:23.504
In a way, what we call the social demand
for history or for memory

00:36:23.504 --> 00:36:27.504
are two different things,
but we see them a lot in societies today.

00:36:27.504 --> 00:36:31.104
I think that, in fact, it is fundamental.
On the one hand, I would say

00:36:31.304 --> 00:36:35.604
that I need, as a researcher —
I speak for myself — as a researcher, I need

00:36:35.604 --> 00:36:39.104
to feel free to make
choices and ask questions.

00:36:39.304 --> 00:36:44.304
So to not feel like I'm being forced
into a research agenda.

00:36:44.304 --> 00:36:50.004
While being completely open to help, guide,
open up avenues, think about questions.

00:36:50.004 --> 00:36:54.004
However, I think that this is something
very important, this freedom to search.

00:36:54.004 --> 00:36:56.004
It is obviously fundamental.

00:36:56.004 --> 00:37:00.704
On the other hand, to counterbalance that,
and to try to answer all the elements brought,

00:37:01.904 --> 00:37:06.704
this research that I am going to carry out,
I take my example, but I mean in an abstract way,

00:37:06.704 --> 00:37:11.404
it also needs this social demand.
If there is no interest,

00:37:11.404 --> 00:37:15.704
if no one cares,
if there is no public funding, if...

00:37:16.004 --> 00:37:21.804
or private foundations even, there you go,
if there is no incentive behind this research,

00:37:21.804 --> 00:37:24.954
and if this research cannot
also produce something for society,

00:37:24.954 --> 00:37:26.404
then it is useless.

00:37:26.404 --> 00:37:30.004
What we are asked
to do is to publish

00:37:30.004 --> 00:37:35.004
in scientific journals, etc.,
but also to contribute

00:37:35.504 --> 00:37:41.203
to debates, to contribute to knowledge
and in all... I would say, in humbleness.

00:37:41.203 --> 00:37:46.204
That is to say, being conscious of the limits
of our own work, because this limit

00:37:46.204 --> 00:37:48.804
can be many things,
it can be the languages we master,

00:37:48.804 --> 00:37:53.004
it can be the possibilities
we have had to consult documents,

00:37:53.004 --> 00:37:57.005
it can be the interpretation
we make of certain things,

00:37:57.005 --> 00:37:58.506
but which nevertheless
brings something.

00:37:58.506 --> 00:38:01.006
So history is not
something static.

00:38:01.006 --> 00:38:04.906
I think it's important to point that out.
It is even more true in subjects like these,

00:38:04.906 --> 00:38:10.656
which are treated by different authorities:
justice, civil society, historians,

00:38:10.656 --> 00:38:14.007
education. Not only teaching
in schools, but also in universities.

00:38:14.007 --> 00:38:15.507
This is also a real question.

00:38:17.007 --> 00:38:19.007
But so...

00:38:19.007 --> 00:38:24.507
It is extremely important that all this
remains connected, but without losing this freedom.

00:38:25.207 --> 00:38:28.307
Without losing this freedom
because I think that...

00:38:28.307 --> 00:38:34.207
then we'll come to the question of expertise.
A historian, a jurist or a sociologist,

00:38:34.207 --> 00:38:39.907
who is called upon in a trial
to shed light on the past

00:38:39.907 --> 00:38:43.407
or on a social issue.
This does exist, of course.

00:38:43.707 --> 00:38:48.807
There are many examples in history, many trials.
It is especially the trials for crimes against humanity,

00:38:48.807 --> 00:38:53.507
for war crimes, for crimes of genocide,
in which we observe all this.

00:38:53.507 --> 00:38:59.001
There, it is again a different role.
However, if we talk about research,

00:38:59.001 --> 00:39:04.601
for me, research
must be part of this society,

00:39:04.801 --> 00:39:09.001
but I would not say by seeking
this neutrality, but on the other hand,

00:39:10.601 --> 00:39:14.501
by seeking how to say that...
a subjectivity that is necessarily there,

00:39:14.501 --> 00:39:18.001
because the questions that we ask,
they are necessarily dictated by many things.

00:39:18.001 --> 00:39:21.901
The discussion we're having today
will undoubtedly, at one point or another,

00:39:21.901 --> 00:39:25.001
lead to a question that I will ask myself
in a research project.

00:39:25.001 --> 00:39:29.001
It's great because
it's obviously a dialogue.

00:39:29.001 --> 00:39:32.501
However, this is
obviously subjective.

00:39:32.501 --> 00:39:35.701
Simply the way in which
one answers this question,

00:39:35.701 --> 00:39:40.002
it must be based on...
I think it should

00:39:40.302 --> 00:39:44.002
be based on all those elements we talked about
at the beginning of the interview.

00:39:44.052 --> 00:39:47.052
That is to say,
the criticism of sources,

00:39:47.352 --> 00:39:53.352
the fact of... not to bringing
preconceived ideas about the sources,

00:39:53.352 --> 00:39:58.202
not to look for that answer,
whatever it may be, or to reinforce that thesis,

00:39:58.202 --> 00:40:01.902
but to be open to elements
that may contradict that thesis.

00:40:02.002 --> 00:40:06.702
Basically, I would say, to respect the voices
that we find in the archives, or the people we interview,

00:40:06.702 --> 00:40:10.702
when we do oral history.
I mean, it's a bit of ethics.

00:40:11.002 --> 00:40:15.002
I don't know, research ethics.
I think that's what's fundamental.

00:40:15.002 --> 00:40:20.002
There, all the tools of the researcher
make it possible to answer these questions,

00:40:20.002 --> 00:40:26.002
which may be subjective,
but which are framed, through these tools.

00:40:26.102 --> 00:40:32.602
So I believe that it is there where the borders
between politics, history and memory are drawn,

00:40:32.602 --> 00:40:36.602
without these borders preventing
the exchange of ideas

00:40:36.602 --> 00:40:38.802
beyond these.
That's very important.

00:40:38.802 --> 00:40:43.802
Then each one defines for himself
what role he wants to play,

00:40:43.802 --> 00:40:47.052
in what role he wants
to play, of course.

00:40:47.952 --> 00:40:50.052
I don't know if this answers to...
because... but don't hesitate...

00:40:50.052 --> 00:40:53.052
Yes, fine.
...if it doesn't.

00:40:53.802 --> 00:40:55.902
Look, this is great.

00:40:55.902 --> 00:41:03.302
Anyway, I think that all the elements
that we discussed here, we can put them...

00:41:03.302 --> 00:41:07.802
well, put them even more
in debate with the...

00:41:07.802 --> 00:41:12.902
when you come to make your presentation
with the members of CaCoBuRwa.

00:41:13.202 --> 00:41:17.502
Thank you very much and...

00:41:18.202 --> 00:41:21.502
and so we'll see you
in a few weeks.

00:41:22.502 --> 00:41:24.002
Thank you very much.

